build/
out/
dist/
*.img
*.iso
# The per-environment kits are not committed either, and for the same reason
# the ISO is not: each carries a copy of the same image, so three of them would
# put roughly sixty megabytes of duplicate content into every clone of this
# repository forever. They are built by scripts/build-vm-packages.sh and
# published as release assets. The single xaios_b<n>.iso.zip stays tracked so
# the release can travel with the repository; three more copies of it earn
# nothing.
release/xaios_b*-qemu.zip
release/xaios_b*-vmware-fusion.zip
release/xaios_b*-virtualization-framework.zip
# The same reasoning for the two hardware kits from build-boot-media.sh. The
# USB kit is a fourth copy of the image. The netboot kit is not a copy of
# anything -- it is the only artifact here that the tracked zip cannot be
# turned into -- but make boot-media rebuilds it byte-for-byte from the tree,
# so tracking it would buy nothing a tag does not already give.
release/xaios_b*-usb.zip
release/xaios_b*-netboot.zip
*.efi
*.elf
*.bin
*.map
*.log
*.dSYM/
.DS_Store
.cache/
__pycache__/
compile_commands.json

# Credentials and secrets.
#
# This repository is heading towards commercial use and will carry financial
# data. Nothing matching these patterns belongs in it. The exceptions at the
# end are deliberate: a throwaway TLS pair the packaging gate needs and the
# public ISRG root certificates the trust store ships. Both are documented in
# tests/fixtures/README.md, and the private key there is public by definition.
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
.env
.env.*
.netrc
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
*_rsa
*_ecdsa
*_ed25519
secrets.*
credentials.*
service-account*.json
!tests/fixtures/xapt-tls-key.pem
!tests/fixtures/xapt-tls-cert.pem
!userspace/apps/trust/*.pem

# Release images are not committed. GitHub refuses any file over 100 MB, and a
# 227 MB ISO in history would be carried by every clone of this repository
# forever. The release note beside them is tracked; the image itself is
# published as a GitHub release asset and rebuilt from a tagged commit with
# make unified-image. Its SHA-256 is recorded in the note, so a downloaded
# image can be checked against what was gated.
release/*.iso
# The zip is tracked. It is about a tenth the size of the image, which puts it
# under GitHub's 100 MB limit, so the release can travel with the repository.
!release/*.iso.zip
